Text: Recognizing Arrow Of Time In The Short Stories Fahimeh Hosseini Hosein Fooladi Shenakht Pajouh / Sharif Shenakht Pajouh / Sharif University of Technology, University of Technology, Tehran, Iran fahim.hosseini.77@ fooladi.hosein@ gmail.com gmail.com Recognizing arrow of time in short stories is a challenging task.

Rank: 6
Source: 1903.10548.pdf
Page: 1
Section: 1 Introduction
Text: Speciﬁcally, pre- trained models such as the OpenAI transformer (Radford et al., 2018) and BERT (Devlin et al., 2018) which are based on transformer architecture, have signiﬁcantly improved accuracy on different benchmarks. In this paper, we are introducing a new dataset which we call ParagraphOrdering, and test the ability of the mentioned models on this newly introduced dataset. We have got inspiration from ”Learning and Using the Arrow of Time” paper (Wei et al., 2018) for deﬁning our task.

Rank: 7
Source: 1903.10548.pdf
Page: 1
Section: 1 Introduction
Text: They sought to understand the arrow of time in the videos; Given ordered frames from the video, whether the video is playing backward or forward. They hypothesized that the deep learning algorithm should have the good grasp of the physics principle (e.g. water ﬂows downward) to be able to predict the frame orders in time. Getting inspiration from this work, we have deﬁned a similar task in the domain of NLP.

Rank: 8
Source: 1903.10548.pdf
Page: 2
Section: 3.2 Fine-tuning BERT
Text: In the case of feature extraction, the network weights freeze and CLS token are fed to the classiﬁer. In the case of ﬁne-tuning, we have used different numbers for maximum sequence length to test the capability of BERT in this task. First, just the last sentence of the ﬁrst paragraph and the beginning sentence of the second paragraph has been used for classiﬁcation. We wanted to know whether two sentences are enough for ordering classiﬁcation or not.

Rank: 9
Source: 1903.10548.pdf
